Z||000004||gixxer||02-15-2001||12:56 AM||||Just find out what the different weights are versus a 2.8 vs. a 3.8. From there you can work out the weight distribution. <BR>---If you are worried about the rear weight bias, put larger tires on the back and/or smaller tires on the front. Use wider rims in the back vs. the front. Use a smaller anti-roll bar on the back and/or a larger or more effective bar on the front. Reletively softer springs in the back and stiffer in the front. Roll center lower in the back and higher in the front.....and so on.<BR>For ANY rearward weight distribution you can come up with, you can make the car handle safely.  You just have to have the knowledge.<BR>Buy a book on vehicle suspension design and you'll understand how to make the car handle. <BR>Trying to add weight to the front (like a battery move) is somewhat counter-productive, as it can't help but make the car steer heavier, but it does tend to make the car understeer as it loads up the front tires..<BR>So I wouldn't get all hot and bothered about the weight distribution, but counter any oversteering tendancies with good old suspension tuning.<BR>BTW, there is no in-depth suspension information available on the internet. So, fork out the cash and buy books! <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||THE BEAST||02-15-2001||03:32 PM||||"Relatively softer springs in the back and stiffer in the front"  - I thought that you had to do it the other way around. I'm confused here. <P>Can anybody here explain this a little be better please. <BR>  <BR>Also: “Roll center lower in the back and higher in the front...and so on.” (Does this mean that the car has to be lower in the back than it does in the front?)<P>  <BR>I'm just trying to get some help here, sorry if I sound like I'm so loud or maybe yelling, but I like my car too much and I don't want anybody to tell me that it doesn’t even handle like a regular sports car. (When for me and many other people in this forum it's one of the nicest sports car ever made).<BR> <BR>||||reg||9
Z||000006||gixxer||02-15-2001||08:00 PM||||Softer springs help the roadholding because they help even out the vertical load "spikes" on a bumpy road, that cause the tires to run at higher slip angles or to loose contact with the ground altogether. <BR>Roll Centers are more abstract.<BR>The roll center (RC)is a geometric reaction point of either the front or the rear suspension. The front or rear RC's are found separately. Put both the front and rear together and you get a line (called the Neutral Roll Axis)passing from the front to the rear, usually at an angle. All the wheel loads in a corner are passed through the respective RCs. If the center of gravity(CG) height is lower than the neutral roll axis(NRA), the car's body will roll to the INSIDE of the turn. IF the CG is above the NRA, the car's body will roll to the outside of a turn.<BR>Now to plagiarize Millikens book:<BR>----------"THE EFFECT OF ROLL CENTER LOCATION WHEN BRAKING OR CORNERING<BR>-Roll center heights front and rear partially determine the way the roll moment on the car from lateral force(cornering) is distributed. Lowering the roll center on one end will lower the roll moment resisted by that end; the wheels on that end will be more evenly loaded in cornering compared to the other end of the car. For example, assume that the car is loose on turn entry; the idea is to sacrifice grip on the front to increase grip on the rear. The outside front is taking  the largest share of the load and increasing the front roll resistance by raising the front roll center will further degrade it to help the rear stick proportionately better. Additional help can be obtained by lowering the rear roll center height as well. It should be noted that "too high" a roll center leads to jacking."<BR>--The quoted stuff is from a suspension set-up table in "Race Car Vehicle Dynamics" by Milliken.  ||||reg||1
